    2	
    3	**Session number:** 79
    4	**Mode:** frontier attack (ATTACK_VECTORS.md §A.A3)
    5	**Channelling:** Bourgain (spectral gap on Cayley graphs of arithmetic
    6	groups, sum-product mixing); Lubotzky-Phillips-Sarnak (arithmetic
    7	Cayley graph spectra).
    8	**Cross-domain ingredient:** spectral graph theory of Cayley graphs of
    9	finite abelian groups (Babai 1979, "Spectra of Cayley graphs":
    10	eigenvalues of Cay(G,S) for abelian G are character sums
    11	λ_χ = Σ_{s∈S} χ(s)).
    12	**Date:** 2026-04-26
    13	**Self-grade: B**
    14	
    15	## Pre-stated falsification criterion
    16	
    17	> H_A (positive, A-grade): there exists a polynomial-time-computable
    18	> scalar feature F(spec(Cay((Z/nZ)*, fixed S))) such that F is in
    19	> **disjoint** ranges for primes n and composites n on a held-out set.
    20	>
    21	> H_0 (closure, B-grade): every feature has overlapping ranges, OR the
    22	> "feature" is a rephrasing of a known primality test.
    23	
    24	## Outcome
    25	
    26	**H_0 confirmed structurally.** No feature is disjoint between primes
    27	and composites. The failure mode is clean: the spectrum probes ω(n)
    28	(number of distinct prime factors), not primality. Specifically (Z/p^kZ)*
    29	is cyclic for odd p, so primes and prime powers produce structurally
    30	identical unit groups, indistinguishable by spectrum.
