    748	| §C1 ATTACK_VECTORS — BK arithmetic-correction probe at Odlyzko's high-height tabulated zeros (zeros4 T~1.4·10²⁰ N=10⁴, zeros5 T~1.4·10²¹ N=10⁴, L=44.6/46.8) — wild-swing extension of S49 | FAIL | I | DUPLICATE-PLUS of line 733 (S49 N=8000 closure at L=7) but with **new quantitative obstruction**. Re-ran the full BK template Pearson + per-prime Fourier amplitude + phase-coherence battery on Odlyzko's published high-height tables. Empirical Pearson(D, BK_template) = +0.063 (zeros4), +0.035 (zeros5). **Proper null** (random-prime template: replace primes with uniform pseudo-primes in [2,50] preserving template shape): null mean = +0.063 / +0.035, std = 2-4·10⁻⁴, **z-scores -0.94σ / +0.93σ** — empirical Pearson is statistically indistinguishable from random-frequency template. Direct test: prime-frequency |Fourier amplitude| median = 0.0141/0.0100 vs random-frequency 0.0148/0.0101 — **prime amplitudes NOT enhanced** (ratios 0.95, 0.99). The previously-reported gap-shuffled-null +33σ "signal" is **null-bias artefact**: gap-shuffled has Poisson-leakage in long-range tail anti-correlated with any oscillatory template, inflating apparent z-scores. **NEW QUANTITATIVE OBSTRUCTION** (sharpens E7.1, E1.10, E3.13): empirical fit `\|BK_pred\|_max · L² ≈ 13.6` (invariant across L=44.6, 46.8, confirms 1/L² scaling), `pair_rms ≈ 4/√N` (verified across N ∈ {2000, 8000, 10000}). Detection threshold: N ≥ (4κ/13.6)² · L⁴ ≈ 0.09 κ² L⁴ for κ-σ detection. **At κ=3: required N at L=44.6 is 3.5×10⁵ zeros** (Odlyzko provides 10⁴ — **35× short**); at L=46.8 is 4.2×10⁵ (**42× short**). At L=80 (T~10⁵²): N≥3·10⁷, hopeless; even at L=20 needs ~10⁵ zeros. **L⁴ scaling is structural**: doubling height L requires 16× more zeros. The asymptotic regime *suppresses* the BK signal faster than data accumulation can compensate. Cross-domain ingredient: Odlyzko's tabulated zeros at heights T ~ 10²⁰-10²¹ (numerical-analysis frontier; precision ~10⁻⁶ in γ). **First quantitative version of "BK is asymptotically too weak" obstruction in the project's catalogue.** Implies any future BK-template detection via direct R₂ residual is bounded below by N_required(L) ≥ 0.81 L⁴ — a hard scaling barrier independent of computational budget. Closes ATTACK_VECTORS §C1 with a structural negative result. Suggested EDGE upgrade: extend E7.1 from qualitative "BK undetected" to quantitative "BK detection requires N ≥ 0.81 L⁴ zeros, infeasible for any tractable L". Cites E7.1, E1.10, E3.13, S49. See experiments/analytic/zeta_structure/odlyzko_high_height/odlyzko_bk_probe_results.md. | 71 |
    749	| Free cumulants of the chi_P MPS unfolding operator — composition C2 of E2.1 + free probability | REFINEMENT | E | Construction succeeded as a moment-level refinement of E2.1 plus a cross-domain import (free probability, Mingo-Speicher 2017); no polylog opening. Built A_chi^pos = M^(j) M^(j)^T as a positive self-adjoint operator under tracial state τ = (1/W^j) tr; computed first 4 free cumulants from non-crossing-partition moment-cumulant relation κ_4 = m_4 − 4m_1 m_3 − 2m_2² + 10m_1² m_2 − 5m_1⁴. Verified MP(c) standardized free-cumulant identity κ_r = c^{r-1} on a Gaussian iid (4000, 1000) ensemble before deployment. Across W ∈ {2, 3, 5, 6, 30}, d up to 22 (W=2), 12 (W=3), 8 (W=5,6), 4 (W=30), the chi_P MPS spectrum decomposes into three regimes: **(i)** finite "structural peak" reproduced by matched-active-Bernoulli baseline (rank-1 mean + φ(W)−1 residue indicators); **(ii)** spike band of `O(N^{0.42})` outlier eigenvalues absent from baseline — adaptive outlier count k*(W,d) defined as smallest k s.t. drop_k bulk free cumulants match MP(c=φ(W)/W) within 10% relative, observed `k*/√rank` ≈ 0.6–1.6 (drifting upward from 0.62 at W=2 d=14 to 1.56 at W=2 d=22; fitting `k* ∝ R^{0.85}`); **(iii)** MP bulk matching `c = φ(W)/W = ∏_{p≤W}(1 − 1/p)` (the **Mertens product**) exactly. Three pre-stated falsification predictions: PR1 (chi_P drop_0 within 10% of MP(0.5)) FAIL at small drop, PASS after adaptive drop; PR2 (active-Bernoulli matches MP) PASS from drop_1; PR3 (chi_P bulk matches MP after finite projection across all W) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) the MP-bulk free-Poisson rate of chi_P's MPS unfolding equals the Mertens product φ(W)/W — a new identification of a free-probabilistic invariant with a sieve density; (b) spike-count regularity `k*(W,d) ∝ R^{0.85}` — a structural fact beyond E2.1's rank statement; (c) verified MP(c) standardized cumulant identity. **Algorithmic implication**: any spectral compression of M^(j) faithful at the second-moment level (i.e., reproducing κ_2) needs rank Ω(N^{0.42}) — recovers a polynomial-in-N barrier from a free-probabilistic angle, parallel to (not stronger than) Lagarias-Odlyzko O(N^{2/3}) and the explicit-formula O(N^{1/2+ε}) bounds. Cites E2.1; cross-domain reference Mingo & Speicher 2017 Ch. 1-2, Ch. 4. See experiments/constructions/free_cumulants_chi_p/. | 74 |
    750	| §A.A3 ATTACK_VECTORS — spectral primality test via fixed-generator (Z/nZ)* Cayley graph (cross-domain: spectral graph theory, Bourgain) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether spec(Cay((Z/nZ)*, {2,3,5,2⁻¹,3⁻¹,5⁻¹})) decides primality of n. Empirical: 533 values n ∈ [7,2000] coprime to 30 (300 primes, 13 prime powers, 214 semiprimes, 6 triple-prime composites); 24 spectral features tested (gap, mults, integer-eigenvalue count, trace moments, max multiplicity, entropy). **No feature is disjoint primes-vs-composites.** Mann-Whitney AUC for primes-vs-prime-powers (the hard sub-case): 0.509 with {2,3,5}, 0.566 with {2,3,7}, 0.673 with {2,3,5,7}∪inv — essentially noise. **Structural reason** (proved from CRT character theory + cross-checked on 342 n × 3 generator sets, 0 violations): for n odd squarefree-or-not coprime to S, n_int_eigs(Cay) ≥ **2^ω(n)** (each (Z/p_i^{a_i}Z)* contributes 2-torsion of order 2; product over distinct prime factors). Lower bound is sharp in 84/533 cases. **Spectrum probes ω(n), not primality**: prime n and prime power n=p^k both have ω=1, so 2^ω = 2, and (Z/p^kZ)* is cyclic for odd p (Gauss), structurally identical to (Z/qZ)* for prime q with appropriately aligned discrete logarithms. (E) Spectral feature reduces to ω(n) extraction; deciding primality from ω(n) requires also a perfect-power test, which inherits AKS-class growing-dim MPOW depth (E5.3, E7.10). **Distinct from prior Cayley graph closures** (CLOSED_PATHS lines 354, 383, 384, 385) which used primes-as-generators (circular); A3 fixed generators and varied n. **Net new content**: (a) explicit lower bound n_int_eigs ≥ 2^ω(n) with character-theoretic proof; (b) negative-shape edge candidate: any fixed-generator abelian Cayley graph spectrum is blind to prime-vs-prime-power; (c) generator-robustness empirically verified across {2,3,5}, {2,3,7}, {2,3,5,7}. Cites E5.3, E5.8, E7.10. See experiments/circuit_complexity/cayley_spectral_primality/. | 79 |
    751	| Tensor-network compression family (MPS / HT / TR / PEPS / CP / Tucker / MERA) — N1 negative-shape closure of E2.1 | REFINEMENT | I | Family-level lift of E2.1 from MPS-only to a uniform half-cut bond-dim lower bound across **five classical polynomial-spatial-locality tensor ansätze**. For each (W, d) pair tested (22 pairs spanning W∈{2,3,4,5,6,7}, d∈{4,6,8,10,12}), measured: (a) MPS half-cut bond dim (E2.1 verification); (b) Hierarchical Tucker root-children bond dim; (c) PEPS 2D-reshape boundary cut rank; (d) CP-rank Kruskal lower bound = max over j of unfolding rank; (e) Tensor Ring bond dim (structurally MPS). **Empirical finding**: all four bond-dim measures are *identical* and equal `min(W^j, φ(W)·W^(d-j-1)+1)` at the maximising cut j=d/2, exactly in 21/22 cases (the one finite-size deficit is W=5 d=4: actual 20 vs predicted 21, a single-row dependency at small N=625). Asymptotic ratio `bond_dim / sqrt(N) → φ(W)/W` (the Mertens product) verified at d=12 for W∈{2,3,4} and at d=8 for W=6: 33/64=0.515 vs 1/2; 487/729=0.668 vs 2/3; 513/1024=0.501 vs 1/2; 433/1296=0.334 vs 1/3. Tucker and MERA close by **orthogonal mechanisms**: Tucker by mode-slice independence (Dirichlet's theorem forces multilinear rank tuple = (W,W,...,W), no compression); MERA by parameter-count (depth log d × bond dim χ^c can't represent N^{1/2} unfolding rank without polynomial χ). **Unifying mechanism**: each ansatz admits an unfolding contraction whose matricisation rank IS the bond / rank parameter; this matricisation rank is bounded below by E2.1. **No falsification fires** on F1-F6 from definition.md. **Net new content**: (i) E2.1 promoted from single-ansatz (MPS) to family-level (5 ansätze + 2 by orthogonal closure); (ii) the Mertens product φ(W)/W is the universal asymptotic compression ratio across the family; (iii) family-level closure subsumes prior single-decomposition CLOSED_PATHS rows (lines 171, 185, 517, 518, 600). **Algorithmic implication**: no polynomial-spatial-locality tensor-network ansatz on chi_P admits polylog bond dim — generalises the Lagarias-Odlyzko sqrt(x) barrier from MPS to all listed ansätze. Cites E2.1 (annotated S77), E1.9, E6.3. See experiments/constructions/tensor_compression_family_closure/. | 77 |
    752	| §D.D4 ATTACK_VECTORS — Szegedy quantum walks on number-theoretic graphs (cross-domain: Szegedy 2004 discriminant matrix theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether Szegedy walks on a number-theoretic graph yield polylog primality extraction. Tested **three families** with the discriminant matrix `D(x,y) = sqrt(P(x,y)·P(y,x))` and the eigenvalue-lifting theorem `cos(θ_k) → e^{±2iθ_k}` (mixing time `O(1/sqrt(δ))`). **(A) Cayley `Cay((Z/NZ)*, {2,3,5,inv})`** for primes N∈{31,…,1009} (14 sizes): empirical `t_classical(N) ~ N^{0.789}`, `t_szegedy(N) ~ N^{0.414}` — quadratic Szegedy speedup empirically realised but neither is polylog. Direct **quantum extension of E7.12 (S79)**: classical Cayley spectrum probes ω(n) not primality, AND Szegedy quantisation inherits the same poly-mixing barrier. **(B) Coprime `C_x = ([1..x], gcd=1)`** for x∈{30,…,1000}: spectral gap is `Ω(1)` (asymptotically `δ → 0.4166...`); stationary prime mass / uniform prime mass `→ ζ(2) = π²/6 ≈ 1.6449` (Mertens; verified at x=1000 with deviation -0.022). Both classical and Szegedy mix in `O(1)`, but bias is constant — `O(log x)` samples to hit a prime + AKS verification = no polylog extractor of π(x). **(C) Divisor `D_x = ([1..x], m|n)`**: gap `~Ω(1)` (slowly decaying 0.21 → 0.166 across x=30..1000). High-prime-mass eigenvectors localise on **prime-centered divisor clusters** `{p, 2p, 3p, ..., 1}` for *individual* primes near x/3-x/2 (e.g., k=14 eigenvector at x=250 is concentrated 100% on {43, 47} ∪ multiples ∪ {1}). Each eigenvector identifies 1-2 specific primes — same structural mechanism as E7.12 (degree class probing), not global primality. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: empirical confirmation `t_Q ≈ sqrt(t_C)` for the Szegedy-Cayley case validates the discriminant theorem at the project's scale; the π²/6 stationary identity is a clean Mertens-law derivation specific to the coprime-graph chain. **Falsification check**: no graph yields mixing time `O(polylog(x))` AND a primality-localised eigenvector with poly-log gap; both Cayley (poly mixing) and coprime/divisor (Ω(1) mixing but no global primality eigenvector) miss the joint condition. Closes ATTACK_VECTORS §D.D4. **Suggested EDGE** (E7.13 candidate): "Szegedy-walk quantisation of any reversible chain on `[1..x]` with adjacency defined by simple arithmetic relation does not yield polylog primality extraction — either spectral gap shrinks polynomially (Cayley class) or eigenvectors capture primality only via degree-class clustering (coprime / divisor class). The two conditions are exhaustive on the families tested." Cites E5.3, E5.8, E7.10, E7.12. Cross-domain reference: Szegedy 2004 (arxiv quant-ph/0401053). See experiments/quantum/szegedy_walk_prime_graphs/. | 80 |
    753	| C6 (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES) — three-pillars × HKM time-space tradeoff classification | REFINEMENT | I | Composition of E7.7 (three-pillars informational closure: prime positions / zeta zeros / floor values) and E6.7 (HKM at (T = N^{8/15}, S = N^{1/3})). Built (alpha, beta) catalog of 14 algorithms (4 prime / 4 zeta / 6 floor) and computed per-pillar Pareto frontiers, cross-pillar dominance, and saturation against E7.6 lower bound (T*S ≥ Ω(N^{5/6}/log N)). **Findings**: (i) HKM is on the floor-pillar Pareto frontier and DOMINATES every other floor-pillar entry elementwise (Lucy DP, Lucy+Fenwick, Meissel-Lehmer classical, Gourdon, primecount); (ii) HKM has NO cross-pillar dominators — its (8/15, 1/3) point is uniquely accessible on the floor pillar (no zero-pillar or prime-pillar entry achieves both T ≤ N^{8/15} AND S ≤ N^{1/3}); (iii) **pillar dominance regions are non-overlapping**: time-only minimum is shared by prime/zeta pillars at α=1/2, space-only minimum is unique to floor at β=1/3, T*S minimum is unique to floor at 13/15 ≈ 0.867; (iv) HKM saturates E7.6 to within N^{0.034} (gap 13/15 - 5/6 = 1/30); (v) Aggarwal binary search (E6.6) is a meta-algorithm whose effective placement migrates with the chosen pi(x) sub-routine. All four pre-stated falsification criteria (F1: HKM dominated; F2: HKM cross-pillar dominated; F3: identical pillar frontiers; F4: floor-pillar T*S < 5/6) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) explicit pillar-by-pillar Pareto map of the project's catalog; (b) HKM uniqueness lemma (HKM's (T, S) point is achievable on the floor pillar ONLY); (c) saturation verdict (floor pillar asymptotically tight). No polylog opening — refinement of E7.7 and E6.7, not a new edge. Cites E5.1, E6.5, E6.6, E6.7, E6.8, E6.9, E7.6, E7.7, E7.9. See experiments/constructions/pillar_tradeoff_diagram/. | 81 |
    754	| §A.A1 ATTACK_VECTORS — SAT/ILP-based search for small TC^0 (depth-2 sign-threshold W=1) circuits computing PRIMES at N≤8 (cross-domain: SAT-based circuit synthesis, Niemetz et al.) | FAIL | I | Wild-swing on §A1: enumerate small TC^0 circuits matching PRIMES at N=8 via SAT/ILP. Built three solvers (Z3 If-then-else, direct ILP via PuLP+CBC, pre-enumerated column-selection ILP); the pre-enumeration approach is 5-10x faster. **Exact PTF degrees** (LP feasibility on monomial basis, real coefficients): PRIMES at N=4..8 → 2, 3, 3, 3, **4**; pi(x) mod 2 → 3, 3, 3, 4, 4; matched random median over 5 seeds → 2, 3, 3, 3, 4. PRIMES PTF degree statistically indistinguishable from random — confirms E1.10/E3.13 at the Boolean-function level. **Exact depth-2 sign-threshold (W=1 both layers) sizes**: N=4 → M=3, N=6 → M=6 (full kmax candidate set, 1458 W=1 thresholds). N=7 inconclusive (M=5, 6 unsat; M=7, 8 timed out at 180s). N=8: M ≥ 17 with kmax≤5 candidate set (M=8, 10, 12, 14 unsat with kmax=4; M=16 unsat with kmax=5; M=20 timed out). **Statistically significant PRIMES-vs-random gap at N=6**: PRIMES needs M=6 bottom gates; ALL 10 random matched controls (weight 18/64) need M ∈ {7, 8} (4 seeds → M=7, 6 seeds → M=8). Binomial null p < 0.001. **First instance in the project of a circuit-complexity measure where PRIMES empirically deviates from a matched-density random function** (35+ prior pseudorandomness measures show no deviation). **Mechanism**: PRIMES has an exceptionally strong single-bit predictor (bit_0 = "is x odd") with 70.3% accuracy at N=8, vs random's best 1-bit at 57.0% (max over 30 seeds). The depth-2 circuit gets the 1-bit predictor "for free" as the first bottom gate; subsequent gates correct fewer remaining errors → smaller M. The PRIMES-vs-random depth-2 gap REDUCES TO the elementary "most primes are odd" fact (1-bit advantage of 0.13 absolute at N=8). At N=4 the gap is absent (random can match) because the small domain admits accidental 1-bit predictors; at N≥6 the gap is robust because no random function of similar weight has such a strong 1-bit bias. **Bottom-layer gates of found solutions are empirical fits**, not number-theoretic primality witnesses (no Miller-Rabin / BPSW / AKS interpretation). **Closure mode I** (information loss): the depth-2 W=1 sign-threshold complexity at N=8 is super-linear in N (M_PRIMES ≥ 17 vs N=8), strongly suggesting exponential or super-polynomial scaling not polylog. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: SAT/ILP search across 35K candidate functions enabled exact bounds infeasible by hand. Refines CLOSED_PATHS line "Nonlinear sieve: bitwise/TC^0" (S14) with quantitative complexity numbers. ATTACK_VECTORS §A1 is **NOT closed by this session** (depth-5 size-2000 enumeration intractable with current tools); the W=1 sign-threshold sub-family is closed at N=8 with the ≥17-gate lower bound. Cites E1.10, E3.13, E5.3, E7.10, S20, S28, novel/pseudorandomness_of_pi.md (now requires footnote: 36th measure DEVIATES with elementary mechanism). See experiments/circuit_complexity/sat_tc0_primes_n8/. | 84 |
    755	| Spike eigenvectors of chi_P MPS unfolding identified as Dirichlet character vectors at small odd primes — C2 sub-arc S82 sharpening of S74 | REFINEMENT | C | Sub-arc closure of S74 (free_cumulants_chi_p): the polynomial-in-N "spike band" of `M^(j) M^(j)^T` eigenvalues found in S74 decomposes structurally as **direct sum of residue-class character subspaces at small odd primes coprime to W**. For each top singular pair `(σ_k, u_k, v_k)`, reconstructed `f_k(n) = σ_k · u_k(i) · v_k(j')` at `n = i·W^{d-j} + j' + 1` and computed centered residue-mod-q energy fraction `E_q(k) = ‖P_q(f_k - mean)‖² / ‖f_k - mean‖²` for q ∈ {2..200}; defined `min_q_conductor(k)` = smallest q achieving 95% of `max_q E_q(k)`. **Empirical pattern at (W=2, d=20, k_*=26)**: spikes 1–2 → conductor 6 (= 2·3, count = φ(3) = 2 ✓); spikes 3–6 → conductor 10 (= 2·5, count = φ(5) = 4 ✓); spikes 7–12 → conductor 14 (= 2·7, count = φ(7) = 6 ✓); spikes 13–20 → conductor 30 (= 2·3·5 cross, count = φ(15) = 8 ✓); spikes 21–25 → conductor ~88 (= 8·11, partial mod-11). Saturation across d ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20}: each prime sector first appears as "transitional" partial at small N then locks in at exactly φ(p) once `N/log N ≫ p · φ(p)`. **PR1 partial-PASS** (refined): max-q energy thresholded at >100× baseline rather than >0.5 absolute. **PR2 PASS**: matched-active-Bernoulli baseline at d=20 has `max_q E_q ≤ 5×10⁻⁴` for all spike candidates; chi_P spikes ≥ 10⁻², ratio ≥ 20×. **PR3 PASS**: visited-q dimension sums to k_* within factor 1.5 (25 observed vs 26 predicted). **Cross-W check (W=6, d=6, k_*=7)**: mod-3 sector ABSENT (W incorporates 3); spikes start at conductor 30 (mod-5, count 4 ✓) then conductor 42 (mod-7, count 6-7 ≈ φ(7)). **Net new content**: (a) precise structural identification of the S74 spike eigenvectors as residue-class character vectors at small primes p ≤ P*(N) with P*(N) ≈ N^{0.21}; (b) PNT-consistent prediction `k_* ≈ N^{0.42} / log N` with explicit per-prime φ(p) accounting; (c) **C-circular collapse of C2's algorithmic implication**: the polynomial-in-N rank barrier from S74 IS the small-modulus residue-class bias content `pi(N; q, a)`, exactly the regime E1.5 saturation governs — the spectral barrier and the information-theoretic barrier are the *same object* viewed from opposite sides. (d) E2.1 annotated S82 with the structural identification. **No polylog opening**: T3 / failure mode C — to compute the spike subspace requires already knowing `pi(N; q, a)` at small q. Cites E2.1 (annotated S74, S77, S82), E1.5, S74 (free_cumulants_chi_p). Cross-domain references: Iwaniec-Kowalski 2004 Ch. 3 (Dirichlet characters), Rubinstein-Sarnak 1994 (Chebyshev's bias = the residue-class bias the spikes quantify). See experiments/constructions/spike_eigenvectors_chi_p/. | 82 |
